/* THE AOT RUNTIME ABI: every helper compiled JavaScript may call, declared once.
 *
 * CT_AOT_HELPER(name, return_type, (parameters), may_throw, may_reenter,
 *               is_safepoint)
 * CT_AOT_COVERS(helper, opcode)          one line per opcode a helper serves
 *
 * PRINCIPLE 11: THE RUNTIME OWNS THIS FILE AND THE COMPILER READS IT. ctbrowser
 * expands it to declare and define the helpers; ctcompile includes the SAME
 * file and expands it to build its descriptor table and the declarations it
 * emits. There is no generator and no dependency - an X-macro rather than
 * TableGen, because generating this into ctbrowser would make a runtime-only
 * build require llvm-tblgen and break Principles 2 and 8.
 *
 * THE POINT IS THAT DRIFT IS A COMPILE ERROR. A renamed, removed or misspelled
 * helper fails to build in ctcompile rather than producing a link error, a
 * crash, or - worst - a silently wrong call.
 *
 * PRINCIPLE 3 IS WHY THE LIST IS SHORT: AOT is a BACKEND of this runtime, not a
 * second JavaScript engine. Every row below delegates to semantics the
 * interpreter already implements. Nothing here reimplements property lookup,
 * conversion, calls, classes, promises, proxies, iterators or modules.
 *
 * THE FLAGS ARE OBLIGATIONS, NOT DESCRIPTIONS:
 *   may_throw     the caller must test the status it returns
 *   may_reenter   this helper can run user JavaScript before it returns
 *   is_safepoint  a collection can happen inside it, so every live value the
 *                 caller holds must be reachable from a root ACROSS the call.
 *                 The collector is precise and walks exactly the roots of
 *                 context::each_root (script/vm.hpp); a value
 *                 living only in a native frame is reachable from NONE of them.
 *
 * TWO TIERS OF FAILURE, and they are not the same thing. raise() sets a flag no
 * try/catch can see - the allocation ceiling, a missing module - and the only
 * correct response is to unwind the whole native chain. thrown_ plus
 * unwind_to_handler is the catchable path, and it completes INSIDE the callee:
 * by the time a helper returns there is no exception in flight, only a frame
 * stack that is shorter than it was. That is why the status vocabulary has no
 * value meaning "threw".
 *
 * COVERAGE: 83 of the 93 opcodes in script/bytecode_opcodes.def are served here.
 * The other ten need no runtime call and are listed at the bottom of this file
 * with the reason. Nothing is uncovered by accident.
 *
 * THE POINT OF ALL THIS IS SPEED, SO READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A ROW.
 *
 * A CALL PER OPCODE WOULD BE AN INTERPRETER WITHOUT THE DISPATCH LOOP - and a
 * slower one, because it would pay a call, a spill and a reload where the run
 * loop pays a jump. This table is a SEMANTIC CONTRACT, not an instruction
 * schedule, and it earns its keep in two different ways on the two backends:
 *
 *   EmitC (Phase 10A, THE PRIMARY BACKEND) emits ordinary C++ that INCLUDES the
 *   runtime headers, so a call to a helper is a call to an inline function the
 *   host compiler sees the body of. At -O2 the fast path - a number that is
 *   already a number, a property that is a plain data slot - inlines away and
 *   the slow path stays a call. That is why EmitC went first: the LLVM path
 *   needs a bitcode archive and LTO to get the same thing.
 *
 *   The LLVM dialect backend (Phases 11-12A) cannot inline across a frozen C
 *   ABI, so for THAT backend these signatures are the boundary and the fast
 *   paths have to be open-coded in the IR before the call is emitted.
 *
 * Which gives the rule for every row here: A HELPER IS THE SLOW PATH. Where a
 * check is a tag test against value's constants, a backend must be free to
 * emit the test and skip the call entirely - which is exactly why the ten
 * uncovered opcodes at the bottom of this file are uncovered, and why
 * jump_if_false is NOT among them.
 *
 * Three rows exist for performance rather than for semantics, and deleting them
 * would be correct and slow:
 *
 *   - the site pointer on the property and call helpers, which is where Phase
 *     26's inline caches attach WITHOUT an ABI break. A register machine gives
 *     `get_prop dst, obj, name` a stable identity, and that identity is the
 *     whole reason the bytecode is a register machine rather than a stack one.
 *   - the literal memos on ct_aot_new_string and ct_aot_new_bigint_literal.
 *     These are NOT an optimisation an image may skip: context::allocate counts
 *     TOTAL LIFETIME allocations against a 40,000,000 ceiling and nothing ever
 *     resets it, so an un-memoised literal in a hot loop reaches an UNCATCHABLE
 *     raise on a program the interpreter runs forever.
 *   - ct_aot_failed, which is one load of one bool so that the failure poll can
 *     sit on back edges and before returns rather than after every allocation.
 *     That is sound because allocate() still returns a valid object past the
 *     ceiling: running on is wasteful, never unsafe.
 *
 * And the standing performance HAZARD, which Phase 4 owns: is_safepoint means
 * the caller's live values must be reachable across the call, and the cheapest
 * way to guarantee that - spill everything to a shadow frame at every call
 * site - would give back much of what compiling won. Precise rooting is Phase
 * 29 for that reason. Do not let the conservative version become the ABI.
 *
 * DERIVED, NOT INVENTED. Every row was proposed against the handler in
 * lib/Script/vm/ that owns its semantics today, then independently refuted by a
 * second reader against the same code, then merged. Where a row disagrees with
 * what an opcode's name suggests, its comment says why.
 *
 * AND CITE BY NAME, NOT BY LINE. A line number in a comment is a fact with an
 * expiry date. Phases 3, 4 and 5 moved several hundred lines of run_loop.cpp,
 * call.cpp and vm.hpp, and the DELEGATES TO citations went stale WHOLESALE:
 * six of them ended up pointing past the end of a file that had shrunk, and
 * many more still land inside their file while naming a handler that has since
 * moved elsewhere in it. `run_loop.cpp's VM_CASE(cell_get)` cannot drift; the
 * line range that row used to carry did, and pointed past the end of the file.
 * (Written out rather than quoted, because the checker below cannot tell a
 * citation from a mention of one - which is the right way round.)
 *
 * ctcompile_def_citations checks the half a machine can see - a citation past
 * the end of the file it names - and the rows repaired for it were rewritten
 * as names rather than given fresh numbers to rot. The other half is on the
 * reader: IF YOU FOLLOW A LINE CITATION HERE AND FIND SOMETHING ELSE, the row
 * is stale rather than wrong about the semantics; the claims were checked, the
 * addresses were not re-checked afterwards.
 */

/* A consumer defines whichever of the two it wants and ignores the other; both
 * are UNDEFINED at the bottom of this file, so every inclusion starts clean and
 * a translation unit can include it twice - once for the helpers and once for
 * the coverage - which is exactly what ctcompile does. Without that, the
 * fallback below survives the first include and collides with the second
 * definition, which is a -Wmacro-redefined error and not an obvious one. */
#ifndef CT_AOT_HELPER
#define CT_AOT_HELPER(name, ret, params, may_throw, may_reenter, is_safepoint)
#endif
#ifndef CT_AOT_COVERS
#define CT_AOT_COVERS(helper, opcode)
#endif


#include "helpers/frames-and-bindings.def"
#include "helpers/values-and-properties.def"
#include "helpers/calls-and-modules.def"
#undef CT_AOT_HELPER
#undef CT_AOT_COVERS
